**Rental yield** is the annual return on an investment property expressed as a percentage of its value. Gross yield is simply annual rent divided by property value. Net yield is more useful: it subtracts all property expenses — management fees, council rates, landlord insurance, maintenance, and strata fees — before dividing by the property value. The difference between gross and net yield is typically 1–2 percentage points, making it critical to use net yield for actual investment decisions.

In Australia's highest-value markets, inner-city Sydney properties commonly yield just **2.5–3.5% gross**, while regional areas and Perth can deliver **5–8% gross**. Higher yields often come with lower capital growth expectations. This calculator shows both yield metrics and your annual pre-tax cash flow — the difference between net rental income and your investment loan interest payments — helping you assess whether the property generates positive or negative cash flow before considering tax benefits from negative gearing.
